WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.050
Chris: When planning any project, it’s
essential that all the information

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:09.309
required is captured and documented well
in advance of the projects start date.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.229
This is the starting point for the
projects development as it can be

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:17.320
used to keep all stakeholders aligned
to the achievement of a single goal.

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:22.380
Creating detailed business requirement
documentation can also assist with

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.900
budgets and time frames for the project.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.120
Leah: To ensure that the business fully
understands the needs of the project

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:33.390
and are able to define objectives
that are attainable, a range of steps

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.670
should be taken during the requirements
gathering process to help define how

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.570
to achieve the project’s objective.

00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:44.950
Defining the business requirements
can be complex as needed to consider

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:48.579
the tasks and goals of the business
as well as the client’s needs.

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:51.949
Chris: The first step would be to
understand who all the stakeholders

00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:51.970
are and what their requirements are.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.620
Leah: Stakeholders are anyone involved
in or affected by the project.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.420
In any project there will be multiple
stakeholders which may include end

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.869
users, shareholders, developers,
system administrators, customers,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.730
marketing teams, sales teams, clients
and relevant regulatory authorities.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.410
There will also be a ‘project manager’
and ‘product owner’ who will analyse

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.910
this information and work with
the various teams and individuals

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:23.400
to develop the project plan.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.810
Chris: You then need to think about
the types of requirements needed,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.530
most of these can be defined as
functional or non-functional.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.039
Functional requirements are usually
set out in detailed ‘Functional

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:41.879
Requirements Documentation’ [FRD], and
a ‘Statement of Work’ [SOW] gives an

00:01:43.089 --> 00:01:46.950
overview of the high-level objectives
and requirements of the product.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.490
Non-functional requirements cover
anything that is not relevant to

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.900
a product’s functionality such as
performance, stability, security,

00:01:56.019 --> 00:01:57.539
and technical specifications.

00:01:58.139 --> 00:02:02.826
Leah: For example, ‘Functional
requirements’ for a website may be the

00:02:02.826 --> 00:02:07.809
ability to process payments, whilst a
‘non-functional requirement’ would be

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:12.730
an ‘SSL certificate’ to ensure that
payments that are processed are secure.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:17.939
There may also be other requirements
such as legal, regulatory or even

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:21.520
additional training for those involved
in the development of the project.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.640
Chris: To make sure the information
within the requirements documents is

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.230
comprehensive and covers the needs of
all stakeholders, it needs to be gathered

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:35.150
correctly, this is known as ‘elicitation
activities’ These may include document

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.490
analysis, brainstorming sessions,
surveys, focus groups, prototyping,

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.410
observations and requirement workshops.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.149
Leah: Businesses my use one or
more different methods to achieve

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.780
the level of detail required.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.800
The amount of information for a single
user client may only involve having

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.420
meetings, whereas developing software
for wider use will require a much

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.360
wider range of elicitation methods.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:07.096
Chris: During the information gathering
stage it is critical to record the results

00:03:07.096 --> 00:03:11.916
of all elicitation activities, such as
a meeting or a brainstorming session.

00:03:12.496 --> 00:03:16.806
If not over time information
may become unclear or confused.

00:03:17.326 --> 00:03:22.006
This can be done by means of a simple word
document, presentation or spreadsheet,

00:03:22.416 --> 00:03:26.955
to sophisticated requirement managements
systems and modelling diagrams.

00:03:27.716 --> 00:03:31.075
Initial brainstorming sessions or
meetings with stakeholders could be

00:03:31.076 --> 00:03:35.056
done on a whiteboard, encouraging
collaboration and creativity.

00:03:36.266 --> 00:03:40.201
Leah: Once the requirements information
is recorded it needs to be reviewed and

00:03:40.211 --> 00:03:44.501
analysed, to make sure everything is
clear and concise with no ambiguity.

00:03:45.001 --> 00:03:49.351
If needed revisions can be made before
being ready to be finalise and implement.

00:03:49.401 --> 00:03:53.351
Chris: The final documentation for
the project will be produced, most

00:03:53.351 --> 00:03:57.511
likely by a technical writer who will
provide a Statement of Work [SOW],

00:03:58.521 --> 00:04:03.960
Functional Requirements Document [FRD]
and Business Requirement Documentation.

00:04:04.591 --> 00:04:08.081
There will also be a range of plain
language documentation produced.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:11.828
Leah: Once the project starts there may
need to be changes to the requirements

00:04:12.248 --> 00:04:15.968
and the ‘requirements documentation’
should show this as the project evolves.

00:04:16.548 --> 00:04:20.738
It is vital these changes or new
developments are incorporated, and

00:04:20.778 --> 00:04:24.908
stakeholders informed, otherwise
the documentation will no longer

00:04:24.938 --> 00:04:29.398
be fit for purpose and the project
could face serious issues or delays.

00:04:30.028 --> 00:04:33.918
Chris: When working in an Agile
environment, any changes will be added

00:04:33.918 --> 00:04:38.508
to the backlog by the product owner, and
those involved in the project will carry

00:04:38.508 --> 00:04:43.048
out backlog refinement at a dedicated
meeting during the current sprint.

00:04:43.808 --> 00:04:47.578
Here the product owner and the team
will discuss the existing priorities

00:04:48.008 --> 00:04:52.068
as well as any items that have been
added, carry out refinement, and

00:04:52.068 --> 00:04:54.268
then prioritise the items accordingly

